body:
After messing around with it a bunch, I can get it to pass through the unmodified `VersionFormatter` after removing underscores and separating the `numver` portion from the rest with a `-` instead of a `.`, for example `4.6.1-stable.arch.linux.14d19694e`. I don't love that as my modifiers likely won't be sufficiently robust for all future formats, so I also modified the `VersionFormatter` to accept a `Complex`/`Mess`, but I'm not sure if that will have knock on effects (all tests pass on my machine though).

I think I prefer the updated `VersionFormatter`, so I'm pushing that, but let me know if you'd prefer I go back to altering the string before passing it along.

For posterity, this is what I ended up with that worked when there were no underscores:

```
    let re = Regex::new(r"(^(\d|\.)+)\.([a-zA-Z].+)").unwrap();
    let ver = match re.captures(version) {
        Some(caps) => caps[1].to_string() + "." + &caps[3].to_string(),
        _ => version.to_string(),
    };
```

